// Long-form, hand-written prompts (commercials, campaigns, workflows, starters).
// Examples marked `append: true` are appended to the master prompt so every
// example is a complete, copy-ready prompt.

const ANALOG_COMMERCIAL = `Hyper-kinetic trendy social media commercial built around the exact product shown in the input image. The subject, brand identity, logo and color palette are all derived directly from the uploaded image — every scene features this exact product with its design, materials, textures, logos and proportions kept perfectly consistent throughout. High-fashion streetwear energy meets experimental music-video editing. Analog-digital hybrid aesthetic: crisp product renders buried under 35mm vintage film grain, dust, scratches, light leaks and gate flicker. Relentless pacing, extreme fast cuts, zero static shots — the camera never stops moving until the final frame.

Opens on an extreme macro of the product's most detailed surface — its texture, stitching, engraving, weave or material grain — light and warped reflections sliding across it, shaky handheld with heavy motion blur. Hard flash-to-white transition as kinetic typography slams in letter by letter, letters stretching and jittering with RGB chromatic aberration split.

A violent whip-pan snaps into a low worm's-eye hero shot of the product floating in a void under harsh black-and-white strobe light, the frame stuttering with datamosh glitch and horizontal scanline tears.

Then a hyper-zoom — the camera rockets from wide to an extreme close-up of the product's signature detail (its logo, emblem, seam, sole, clasp, cap — whatever defines it) in one speed-ramped move, hyper-speed collapsing into ultra slow motion at impact, motion blur streaking, five ghosted clone-trail copies of the product trailing behind the movement.

The product's dominant silhouette match-cuts into a fast FPV drone orbit above it, dutch-tilted, rotating aggressively; the grain intensifies and the colors flash-invert into negative for a split second before snapping back.

The frame shatters into a layered 2.5D parallax collage — cutout photos of the product at different scales sliding at different depths, torn-paper edges, halftone textures, scribbled hand-drawn annotations, stamped bold type. The layers separate and drift apart in 3D space.

Product explosion: the product bursts apart in ultra slow motion into its individual components — panels, layers, fabric, hardware, sole, laces, cap, whatever it is physically made of — suspended mid-air as an exploded-view constellation while a shaky handheld orbit drifts through the debris with overlapping echo ghost frames — then time reverses and every part violently snaps back together on a hard speed ramp.

The reassembled product liquid-morphs into giant chrome typography of the brand name taken from the image, letters wobbling like liquid metal, the entire frame stretching horizontally like taffy before snapping back into place.

A strobe montage of near-subliminal flashing images follows — the product worn or displayed on a chrome mannequin silhouette, harsh direct-flash paparazzi style, mid-spin freezes, inverted-color frames, glitch-corrupted frames, VHS tracking errors — stitched together with whip-pans and flash transitions.

Finale: hard cut to black, one last flash, then the product lands dead-center in a razor-sharp static freeze frame, grain still breathing, as the brand name stamps in with a punch animation and holds to the end.

Typography throughout: oversized bold condensed sans-serif mixed with distressed typewriter overlays — slam-ins, per-letter jitter, tracking expansion, wave distortion, rapid position glitches — short punchy words matching the brand's attitude.

Grade: high-contrast desaturated base with neon accent pops sampled from and complementing the product's own brand colors, heavy vintage grain, dust and hair artifacts, light leaks, sprocket-hole frame edges, chromatic aberration on every fast move, VHS wobble on glitch moments, intentional exposure imperfections and focus breathing.

Sound design only, no music: sub-bass impacts on every cut, whooshes on whip-pans, camera shutter clicks on strobe flashes, material-true sounds on the explosion and reassembly (fabric snaps, metal clinks, plastic clicks — matching what the product is made of), tape-stop screeches on speed ramps, static bursts on glitches, an air-tension riser into the logo, then total silence and one final impact hit.

The product's design, logo and details must never warp, change or lose accuracy. No realistic human faces — silhouettes or chrome mannequins only. No smooth gimbal moves, no calm moments before the ending, no watermark, no music.`;

const NEON_COMMERCIAL = `Hyper-kinetic trendy social media commercial built around the exact product shown in the input image. The subject, brand identity, logo and color palette are all derived directly from the uploaded image — every scene features this exact product with its design, materials, textures, logos and proportions kept perfectly consistent throughout. Cyber-digital, holographic-tech energy meets glitch-art motion design. Digital-native aesthetic: crisp product renders fused with scanline interference, chromatic RGB splits, pixel-sort corruption, and holographic projection artifacts — no film grain, no analog dust, purely digital-signal degradation. Relentless pacing, extreme fast cuts, zero static shots — the camera never stops moving until the final frame.

Opens on an extreme macro of the product rendered as a rotating wireframe/holographic scan — thin blue-white scan lines sweeping across its surface like a 3D scanner reading it into existence, edges flickering between wireframe and full-color render. Hard digital "glitch-snap" transition as the scan completes and kinetic typography slams in as pixelated blocky text that resolves into sharp vector type, RGB channels splitting apart and snapping back together.

A violent snap-zoom pushes into a high-angle hero shot of the product suspended inside a rotating holographic grid-cube, neon grid-lines projecting off its surface, the frame stuttering with screen-tear artifacts and horizontal digital noise bursts.

Then a data-warp zoom — the camera rockets through a tunnel of scrolling code/UI fragments and neon light trails directly into an extreme close-up of the product's signature detail (logo, emblem, seam, cap, sole — whatever defines it), the image pixel-sorting and dissolving into streams of falling digital particles before instantly reassembling in crisp full resolution.

The product's silhouette match-cuts into a rapid multi-angle "bullet-time" spin — dozens of frozen camera angles firing in rapid sequence around the product as if captured by a ring of simultaneous cameras, each frame flashing with a different neon color wash before the spin resolves into fluid motion again.

The frame fractures into a holographic UI dashboard collage — floating translucent panels displaying the product from different angles, wireframe schematics, glowing data readouts and stat callouts (no real numbers/claims — abstract UI dressing only), all panels drifting and rotating at different depths in 3D space like a HUD interface.

Digital deconstruction: the product dissolves into thousands of voxel/particle fragments in ultra slow motion, each fragment glowing faintly and drifting outward into a suspended constellation, camera drifting through the particle field with light trails smearing behind each fragment — then the particles reverse-collapse on a hard speed ramp, snapping back into the fully-formed, perfectly accurate product with a bright flash of light.

The reassembled product liquid-morphs into massive glowing neon typography of the brand name, the letters constructed from flowing light-streams and circuitry-like glowing lines, briefly glitching between three different neon color states before locking into the brand's actual colors.

A strobe montage follows — the product displayed on a rotating holographic pedestal, hard directional light sweeps, freeze-frames on dramatic angles, brief frames of inverted/negative color, digital corruption blocks, quick flashes of scrolling binary/UI noise — stitched together with snap-zooms and hard flash transitions.

Finale: hard cut to black, a single sharp digital "power-on" flash, then the product locks into a razor-sharp static hero freeze frame, faint scan-line still breathing across it, as the brand name stamps in with a sharp mechanical snap animation and holds to the end.

Typography throughout: oversized bold geometric sans-serif with subtle circuit/tech detailing — slam-ins, glitch-flicker, RGB split jitters, scanline wipes, rapid scale-pulses — short punchy words matching the brand's attitude.

Grade: cool-toned high-contrast base (deep blacks, electric neon accents) sampled from and complementing the product's own brand colors, crisp digital clarity broken by intentional scanlines, chromatic aberration on fast moves, pixel-sort glitches on transitions, subtle lens-flare blooms from neon light sources, no film grain or analog artifacts anywhere.

Sound design only, no music: sharp digital "blip" hits on every cut, electronic whooshes on snap-zooms, soft synth pulses on scan reveals, material-true sounds on the deconstruction and reassembly (fabric snaps, metal clinks, plastic clicks — matching what the product is made of) layered under a subtle digital resonance, glitch-static bursts on corruption moments, a rising synth tension riser into the logo, then total silence and one final sharp power-on hit.

The product's design, logo and details must never warp, change or lose accuracy. No realistic human faces — silhouettes or holographic projections only. No smooth gimbal moves, no calm moments before the ending, no watermark, no music, no film-grain/analog textures (this style is strictly clean-digital, not vintage).`;

const POSTER_3D = `Create a high-impact, premium 10-second 3D commercial from the uploaded poster.

The uploaded poster is the only source of truth. Keep the product, packaging, logo, text, typography, colors, and proportions exactly as shown. No redesigns, no invented features, no new or altered words/numbers/prices.

Concept: The poster physically breaks apart and the product bursts fully into 3D space — not a parallax effect, not a zoom, not a simple animation. The product must visibly cross the original 2D plane and become a full-scale, physically real object with visible depth, sides, and materials.

Shot-by-Shot Timing
0–2s | Breakout — Flat poster, camera pushes in fast. Poster splits into dimensional layers as the product punches through the surface toward camera, extending well past the original plane. Reveal its 3D form immediately; camera keeps moving, doesn't settle.
2–4s | Hero Reveal — Smooth orbit or dolly around the now-3D product. Large, dominant in frame. Show real materials, textures, reflections, and details invisible in the flat poster.
4–6s | Macro Detail — Fast push into an extreme close-up of the product's most compelling detail (condensation, metal, fabric, texture, liquid, glossy label — whichever fits the actual product). Shallow depth of field, rack focus, luxury-commercial macro photography.
6–8s | Dynamic Turn — Sharp angle change — low-angle push, fast fly-around, overhead reveal, or camera passing through a poster graphic element — chosen to match the product's energy. Original poster elements may now exist as 3D layers in the environment.
8–10s | Final Hero Shot — Camera settles into a premium final composition. Bring back the key logo/headline/promo text as a dimensional graphic layer, exact wording preserved. End on a polished, ad-final frame.

Visual Style: Photorealistic 3D rendering, cinematic lighting and shadows, premium reflections, deep parallax, controlled motion blur, shallow DOF with rack focus, strong perspective, professional ad pacing. Every few seconds must reveal a new angle, scale, or detail — the camera never goes static.

Tone Matching (pick based on poster's message)
Refreshment → crisp, cool product moment
Luxury → slow, elegant, premium photography
Energy → fast, dynamic camera work
Taste → appetizing texture emphasis
Performance → emphasize form and motion
Launch/promo → build anticipation, preserve promo text

Hard Constraints: No weak 2.5D/parallax-only effect · no plain zoom · no generic template animation · no invented products or features · no altered text, numbers, prices, or claims · no random VFX or cartoon physics · no redesigned poster.`;

const ELEMENTAL = `Product image/reference is the only source of truth for final form, packaging, logo, and text. Raw elements used in the buildup must be visually/thematically relevant to the actual product's ingredients or materials — do not invent unrelated elements.

Concept: Raw natural elements connected to the product (citrus, botanicals, water, metal, fabric fibers, grain, etc., matched to the actual product) swirl through 3D space and assemble into the finished product, with lighting shifting from raw/natural to polished/commercial as it forms.

Shot-by-Shot Timing
0–2s | Elements in Motion — Raw, relevant elements (chosen based on the real product) swirl and drift through a dark or softly lit void, camera drifting slowly among them, natural/moody lighting.
2–4s | Convergence Begins — Elements begin accelerating and pulling toward a central point, camera pulls back to reveal the silhouette of the product beginning to take shape from the swirling material.
4–6s | Assembly — Elements snap/flow into place rapidly, forming the exact product — packaging, label, and color detail resolving as the last elements lock in. Camera orbits during this assembly for dimensionality.
6–8s | Lighting Shift — As assembly completes, lighting transitions from raw/natural (moody, directional) to clean commercial studio lighting. Camera pushes into a 3/4 hero angle, reflections and surface detail sharpen.
8–10s | Final Hero Shot — Camera settles on a polished, premium hero composition. Logo and original text appear cleanly, exact wording preserved. End on a still, high-impact ad frame.

Visual Style: Photorealistic particle/element simulation, dramatic directional lighting during buildup transitioning to soft premium studio lighting at reveal, smooth continuous camera movement, physically believable material behavior (no cartoon physics), cinematic depth and atmosphere.

Hard Constraints: Elements used must be relevant to the real product (no random/unrelated objects), no invented packaging or features, no altered text/numbers/claims, no jump cuts, transformation must look physically grounded even if stylized.`;

const FLYING_CAR = `SCENE SETUP
The camera is positioned inside a high-rise apartment, looking through a wide-open window at a busy Manhattan-style avenue from approximately 30 floors above street level. Tall skyscrapers, glass buildings, taxis, crosswalks, and distant traffic fill the background. A grey-painted window frame and stone ledge occupy the foreground.
Overcast daylight, realistic city haze, natural shadows, and subtle handheld iPhone camera shake.

BRAND AND PRODUCT LOCK
The uploaded product image is the absolute source of truth for the brand and the product. Identify the product, brand name, logo, color palette, typography, and packaging from the image. Everything in the video is built from them:
The car is a sleek futuristic electric sports car whose body is painted in the brand's main color, with accent stripes in the brand's secondary color, a black panoramic glass roof, and large black aerodynamic wheels. The exact brand logo from the image appears large and prominent on both doors, with the brand name in clean lettering beside it, and a smaller logo on the bonnet.
The driver is a friendly young male delivery driver wearing a polo shirt or uniform in the brand's colors with matching trim, and a cap with the small brand logo.
The product is the exact product shown in the image, with the same packaging, shape, colors, labels, and logo, and it is handed over exactly as it appears in the image. Do not redesign the packaging, invent new products, or change the logo or any text.

0–3 SECONDS: FLYING CAR ARRIVES
The branded futuristic car flies up the avenue toward the camera. Powerful thrusters underneath blast white steam downward. The vehicle approaches the building, decelerates naturally, and positions itself just outside the open apartment window, with the branded side facing the camera.

3–4 SECONDS: HOVERING AND WINDOW LOWERING
The car stabilizes in midair a few inches beyond the stone window ledge, its body gently bobbing as the thrusters maintain balance. White steam flows downward between the skyscrapers. The driver remains seated inside. The driver-side power window lowers smoothly until fully open. The car door stays completely closed.

4–7 SECONDS: PRODUCT HANDOFF
The driver stays seated and reaches naturally through the lowered window. His torso, legs, and body remain inside the vehicle. He never opens the door, steps outside, or climbs out.
He carefully holds the product and its packaging, exactly as shown in the uploaded image, with the logo facing the camera, and extends it toward the apartment. A bare human forearm and hand enter from the bottom of the frame, representing the camera operator, reach outside the window, and securely grasp the product. The driver releases it only after the receiving hand has a firm grip. Show a clear, physically realistic hand-to-hand transfer. The packaging keeps its shape, any contents stay inside, and nothing floats or morphs.

7–8 SECONDS: DRIVER'S REACTION
The driver smiles and gives a friendly thumbs-up through the open window, then raises the power window smoothly until fully closed. The door remains shut and the brand logo stays visible on the door panel.

8–10 SECONDS: FLYING DEPARTURE
With the driver still inside and the window closed, the thrusters release a powerful burst of white steam. The car tilts slightly away from the apartment and accelerates smoothly down the avenue between the skyscrapers, shrinking into the distance with its branding still visible. The camera stays inside the apartment in the same continuous POV, with the delivered product in the foreground.

VISUAL STYLE AND PHYSICS
Ultra-photorealistic live-action look, realistic iPhone footage, natural overcast daylight, glossy automotive reflections, believable vehicle scale and hovering, consistent car proportions, realistic human anatomy, natural hand movements, detailed material textures on the packaging, volumetric steam, cinematic motion blur, natural shadows, and physically plausible acceleration. Keep the character, vehicle, product, perspective, and lighting consistent throughout the 10-second shot.

AUDIO
Distant city traffic, wind outside the window, low futuristic engine hum, thruster whooshes, the electric power window moving, and subtle packaging rustle during the handoff.

STRICT NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS
No cuts, transitions, or camera teleportation. No collision with the building or window. No opening of the car door. No driver exiting the car, standing outside, or leaning his body outside. No delivery through an open door. No floating packages, extra fingers, deformed hands, duplicate vehicles, warped packaging, misspelled or changed branding, invented logos or brand names, mismatched brand colors, morphing objects, cartoon effects, or unrealistic physics.

ABSOLUTE RULE: The delivery driver must remain seated inside the car for the entire video. Only the driver-side power window moves down and up. The car door stays closed from beginning to end.`;

